    {
      "id": "FKVj-DxqOwyBtsRC5OeCM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5661.671410282486,
      "y": 5334.505037103729,
      "width": 790.0994873046875,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4V",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1499159652,
      "version": 55,
      "versionNonce": 2026069596,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786442105401,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "The current strategy is a hard recent-message window — the last N messages,\nnothing older. Blunt on purpose. It fixes unbounded growth, which is the\nfailure that actually happens, without the machinery of summarization. That was\nthe rejected alternative, and it trades one failure for a worse-behaved one: a\nsummarizer decides what to discard, silently, and when it discards the wrong\nthing the model behaves as though something never happened. Running out of\nwindow is at least loud. Losing the start of a long conversation is the accepted\ncost here, and it is accepted because it is *comprehensible* — \"we keep the last\ntwenty messages\" is something a user can be told.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "The current strategy is a hard recent-message window — the last N messages,\nnothing older. Blunt on purpose. It fixes unbounded growth, which is the\nfailure that actually happens, without the machinery of summarization. That was\nthe rejected alternative, and it trades one failure for a worse-behaved one: a\nsummarizer decides what to discard, silently, and when it discards the wrong\nthing the model behaves as though something never happened. Running out of\nwindow is at least loud. Losing the start of a long conversation is the accepted\ncost here, and it is accepted because it is *comprehensible* — \"we keep the last\ntwenty messages\" is something a user can be told.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "3I-JSijdK_NFXfOUbXiWu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6507.226965838035,
      "y": 5385.060592659288,
      "width": 933.0193481445312,
      "height": 75,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1971c2",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4b",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 476338788,
      "version": 54,
      "versionNonce": 849703396,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786463021320,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "    recent = history[-window_size:] if window_size > 0 else []\n    messages = [{\"role\": item.payload[\"role\"], \"content\": item.payload[\"text\"]} for item in recent]\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user_text})",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "    recent = history[-window_size:] if window_size > 0 else []\n    messages = [{\"role\": item.payload[\"role\"], \"content\": item.payload[\"text\"]} for item in recent]\n    messages.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user_text})",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "QGIOAnCnufCAXryJ3-haR",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5663.266648377828,
      "y": 5622.909799008476,
      "width": 813.1594848632812,
      "height": 250,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#2f9e44",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b4l",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1104306524,
      "version": 62,
      "versionNonce": 45764324,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1786463060152,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Memory is surfaced through ``system``, never appended to ``messages``. Merged\ninto the message list it would be indistinguishable from something the user\nactually said, and the model would treat a preference the system chose to\npreserve as a statement someone made this turn.\n\nMemory is bounded too, and by the same reasoning as the message window. It was\nbriefly not: every entry was rendered, so the one channel the window did not\nwatch was the one that could overflow it. The bound is on the read side, most\nrecent first, so it is describable the same way — \"the twenty most recent\nmemories\" is something a user can be told.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Memory is surfaced through ``system``, never appended to ``messages``. Merged\ninto the message list it would be indistinguishable from something the user\nactually said, and the model would treat a preference the system chose to\npreserve as a statement someone made this turn.\n\nMemory is bounded too, and by the same reasoning as the message window. It was\nbriefly not: every entry was rendered, so the one channel the window did not\nwatch was the one that could overflow it. The bound is on the read side, most\nrecent first, so it is describable the same way — \"the twenty most recent\nmemories\" is something a user can be told.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "QccyeJuyDNcxERzoZSlBM",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 5677.552362663534,
      "y": 5922.909799008459,
      "width": 811.5994262695312,
      "height": 950,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5L",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1546154972,
      "version": 57,
      "versionNonce": 1154663772,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "eG-k0BVO0hlc_ogRBAdUO",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1786463451150,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Not built:\n    Retrieval over external evidence. There is no external source — no\n    documents, no search, no vector store — so there is nothing to retrieve.\n    When one exists, it plugs in here as an *additional section*, and it must\n    arrive as candidate evidence rather than authority: assembled context is a\n    claim about what is relevant, and a retrieved passage carries no more weight\n    than the retriever's confidence in it.\n\n    Relevance ranking over memory. Distinct from the above, and easy to conflate\n    with it: this one adds no section, it replaces how :func:`_format_memory`\n    chooses. Memory is selected by recency today, so a session's twentieth most\n    recent fact is invisible on the turn it finally matters. Ranking the entries\n    against the incoming message instead is what would make this semantic memory\n    by mechanism and not only by role — see the note in\n    :mod:`bacteria.session.store`. Nothing structural is in the way: this\n    function already receives ``user_text``, which is the query, and uses it\n    only to append the new message.\n\n    The reason it is not built is a tension rather than a missing dependency.\n    Ranking makes a memory's absence *silent and query-dependent* — the same\n    turn, phrased differently, surfaces a different set, and a fact the owner\n    deliberately preserved can vanish with nothing reporting it. That is the\n    failure `ADR 0010` rejected when it declined summarization, and rejecting it\n    for messages while accepting it for memory would be inconsistent: a memory\n    was kept on purpose, so losing one silently is worse than losing an old\n    message. Recency is blunter and fails predictably, which is the same trade\n    the message window makes. Whoever builds this has to answer what happens to\n    a preserved fact that did not score well, and \"it was not relevant\" is not\n    an answer the owner who wrote it can check.\n\n    Summarization and compaction. Would replace the window with\n    summary-of-old-turns plus recent turns. Needed once conversations\n    routinely exceed the window; a summarizer must then answer for what it\n    dropped, which is why it is not built speculatively.\n\n    Token-aware budgeting. The window counts *messages*, not tokens, so twenty\n    long messages cost far more than twenty short ones. A real budget would\n    measure tokens and trim to fit, which requires a tokenizer per provider.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Not built:\n    Retrieval over external evidence. There is no external source — no\n    documents, no search, no vector store — so there is nothing to retrieve.\n    When one exists, it plugs in here as an *additional section*, and it must\n    arrive as candidate evidence rather than authority: assembled context is a\n    claim about what is relevant, and a retrieved passage carries no more weight\n    than the retriever's confidence in it.\n\n    Relevance ranking over memory. Distinct from the above, and easy to conflate\n    with it: this one adds no section, it replaces how :func:`_format_memory`\n    chooses. Memory is selected by recency today, so a session's twentieth most\n    recent fact is invisible on the turn it finally matters. Ranking the entries\n    against the incoming message instead is what would make this semantic memory\n    by mechanism and not only by role — see the note in\n    :mod:`bacteria.session.store`. Nothing structural is in the way: this\n    function already receives ``user_text``, which is the query, and uses it\n    only to append the new message.\n\n    The reason it is not built is a tension rather than a missing dependency.\n    Ranking makes a memory's absence *silent and query-dependent* — the same\n    turn, phrased differently, surfaces a different set, and a fact the owner\n    deliberately preserved can vanish with nothing reporting it. That is the\n    failure `ADR 0010` rejected when it declined summarization, and rejecting it\n    for messages while accepting it for memory would be inconsistent: a memory\n    was kept on purpose, so losing one silently is worse than losing an old\n    message. Recency is blunter and fails predictably, which is the same trade\n    the message window makes. Whoever builds this has to answer what happens to\n    a preserved fact that did not score well, and \"it was not relevant\" is not\n    an answer the owner who wrote it can check.\n\n    Summarization and compaction. Would replace the window with\n    summary-of-old-turns plus recent turns. Needed once conversations\n    routinely exceed the window; a summarizer must then answer for what it\n    dropped, which is why it is not built speculatively.\n\n    Token-aware budgeting. The window counts *messages*, not tokens, so twenty\n    long messages cost far more than twenty short ones. A real budget would\n    measure tokens and trim to fit, which requires a tokenizer per provider.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "wgxvxbXsp1qE04w6AyCBh",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 6876.1237912349625,
      "y": 6214.33837043703,
      "width": 830.0194702148438,
      "height": 625,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#e03131",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b5M",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 363018724,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 1148067420,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [
        {
          "id": "eG-k0BVO0hlc_ogRBAdUO",
          "type": "arrow"
        }
      ],
      "updated": 1786463451150,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "def _format_memory(state: SessionState, limit: int) -> tuple[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Render the most recent memory entries as a system prompt.\n\n    Returns the rendered text and how many entries went into it. The count is\n    returned rather than left to the caller because the bound is applied here;\n    a caller counting ``state.memory`` would report what exists rather than\n    what was shown, and those differ by exactly the thing the limit does.\n\n    Each line carries its ``reason`` alongside its value. That is provenance\n    for the model as much as for us: a fact plus why it was kept is something\n    the model can weigh, where a bare assertion can only be obeyed.\n\n    Sorted ascending and trimmed from the front, rather than sorted descending\n    and reversed. The two differ on ties: Python's sort is stable, so ascending\n    keeps entries that share a timestamp in insertion order, where reversing a\n    descending sort would flip them. Several memories written in one request do\n    share a timestamp, so this is the ordinary case, not a corner one — and an\n    order that changes between turns is an unnecessary difference in the prompt.\n    \"\"\"\n    if limit <= 0:\n        return \"\", 0\n    oldest_first = sorted(state.memory.values(), key=lambda entry: entry.created_at)\n    shown = oldest_first[-limit:]\n    lines = [f\"- {e.value} (reason: {e.reason})\" for e in shown]\n    return \"Known context about this user/session:\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(lines), len(shown)",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "def _format_memory(state: SessionState, limit: int) -> tuple[str, int]:\n    \"\"\"Render the most recent memory entries as a system prompt.\n\n    Returns the rendered text and how many entries went into it. The count is\n    returned rather than left to the caller because the bound is applied here;\n    a caller counting ``state.memory`` would report what exists rather than\n    what was shown, and those differ by exactly the thing the limit does.\n\n    Each line carries its ``reason`` alongside its value. That is provenance\n    for the model as much as for us: a fact plus why it was kept is something\n    the model can weigh, where a bare assertion can only be obeyed.\n\n    Sorted ascending and trimmed from the front, rather than sorted descending\n    and reversed. The two differ on ties: Python's sort is stable, so ascending\n    keeps entries that share a timestamp in insertion order, where reversing a\n    descending sort would flip them. Several memories written in one request do\n    share a timestamp, so this is the ordinary case, not a corner one — and an\n    order that changes between turns is an unnecessary difference in the prompt.\n    \"\"\"\n    if limit <= 0:\n        return \"\", 0\n    oldest_first = sorted(state.memory.values(), key=lambda entry: entry.created_at)\n    shown = oldest_first[-limit:]\n    lines = [f\"- {e.value} (reason: {e.reason})\" for e in shown]\n    return \"Known context about this user/session:\\n\" + \"\\n\".join(lines), len(shown)",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
